---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { DIMENSIONS, PROFILES, RUBRIC_VERSION } from '../lib/rubric';
---

<Layout title="How places are scored — Work in Ozaukee">
  <h1>How places are scored</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Six things, scored 1 to 5, every place judged the same way. This page exists so you
    can check my work — if you think a score is wrong, you can point at the exact line
    you'd have picked instead.
  </p>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      A place gets scored once, not every visit — outlets, seating, room noise and whether
      anyone minds you taking a call are properties of the room, not of a Tuesday. When a
      place changes, or I've been enough times to think I got it wrong, I re-score it and
      the date updates. The individual visits are still there to read for how it actually
      went on a given morning.
    </p>
    <p>
      Anchors are written at 1, 3 and 5. I use 2 and 4 for "between these two." The words
      are the point: picking the sentence that matches keeps a 4 in November meaning the
      same thing as a 4 in March.
    </p>
  </div>

  {
    DIMENSIONS.map((d) => (
      <>
        <h2>{d.label}</h2>
        <p class="note">{d.question}</p>
        <ul class="stack">
          <li><b>1</b> — {d.anchors[1]}</li>
          <li><b>3</b> — {d.anchors[3]}</li>
          <li><b>5</b> — {d.anchors[5]}</li>
        </ul>
      </>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Why there's no single star rating</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Because the honest answer to "is this place good?" is "for what?" A wine bar that's
      hopeless for a video call can be the best room in town for three hours of writing.
      Averaging those into one number throws away the only part that matters.
    </p>
    <p>So the same six scores get read three ways:</p>
  </div>

  <ul class="cards">
    {
      PROFILES.map((p) => (
        <li class="card">
          <h3>{p.label}</h3>
          <p>{p.blurb}</p>
          <ul class="attrs">
            {Object.entries(p.weights).map(([dim, w]) => (
              <li>
                {dim} <b>{Math.round((w as number) * 100)}%</b>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      If I haven't scored enough of what a profile needs, it says so instead of showing a
      number. A confident-looking "4.6" built from one answer is worse than an admission
      that I didn't check.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Rubric version {RUBRIC_VERSION}. Every scored visit records which version it was
      judged under, so if I ever rewrite these anchors, old entries stay comparable to
      each other rather than being quietly mixed in with new ones.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
